---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { criteria, questions } from "../../data/program";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// Current Work page (A3g §6). Route stays /research; the displayed H1 and title
// are "Current Work". Frames the modality project as the current case study.
const currentProgram = [projects.threeWays, projects.thesis];
const background = [projects.protobias, projects.latentControl];
---

<Base
  title="Current Work · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="Research on linguistic meaning and inference: formal analysis, a proposed human study, completed model evaluation, and mechanistic interpretability, with their current status."
>
  <article class="cw">
    <h1 class="cw-h1">Current Work</h1>
    <p class="cw-lede">
      My work asks how linguistic meaning guides inference and how to identify the information
      used by people and language models. The projects below contribute different forms of evidence:
      completed model evaluation, formal analysis in development, a proposed human study, and
      methods training in mechanistic interpretability.
    </p>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">The linguistic problem</h2>
      <p class="cw-p">Consider three answers to the question “Where is the key?”</p>
      <ol class="cw-ex">
        <li>It <em>might</em> be in the drawer.</li>
        <li>It <em>might</em> be in the drawer, or it might not.</li>
        <li>I <em>don’t know whether</em> it is in the drawer.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="cw-p">
        These expressions are often grouped under “uncertainty”, but they need not do the same
        discourse work. A possibility claim is compatible with <em>p</em>; a bilateral statement
        explicitly leaves both answers open; an ignorance report concerns the speaker’s knowledge.
        My current paper asks how their commitments and response patterns differ, including when
        the knowledge report conveys an additional lack of a settled opinion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">What would count as evidence</h2>
      <p class="cw-p">
        For the proposed behavioural study, a claim about the later availability of a possibility
        needs more than a general change in response speed. Three diagnostic criteria guide the design:
      </p>
      <ul class="cw-crit">
        {criteria.map((c) => (
          <li>{c.name}: {c.text}.</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="cw-p">
        Evidence-sensitivity is crucial. If a possibility remains active even after it has been ruled
        out, the effect may be simple perseveration rather than semantic maintenance. The design
        would hold the available information fixed, vary the linguistic framing, and check the
        intended interpretation. These are design criteria, not established findings.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">Two empirical questions</h2>
      <div class="cw-qs">
        {questions.map((q) => (
          <div class="cw-q">
            <p class="cw-q-label">{q.label}</p>
            <p class="cw-q-text">{q.text}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="cw-p">
        These questions require different evidence. Human studies can test how the distinction
        affects comprehension, reasoning, and learning. Controlled model studies ask what a
        particular linguistic distribution can support in a particular learner. A language model is
        useful for the second question; it is not a proxy for a person.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">Current status</h2>
      <p class="cw-status">
        The semantic analysis and diagnostic materials are in development, with independent
        judgments pending. The master’s thesis focus is under discussion. A proposed
        adult experiment has not begun. A preliminary model-scoring pipeline runs, but its checks
        have not established the target semantic effect.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">Work within the current case study</h2>
      <div class="cw-projs">
        {currentProgram.map((p) => (
          <div class="cw-proj cw-proj-current">
            <a class="cw-proj-title" href={p.href}>{p.title}</a>
            <p class="cw-proj-meta">{p.status}</p>
            <p class="cw-proj-question">{p.question}</p>
            <p class="cw-proj-desc">{p.summary} {p.connection}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="cw-dep">
        The formal project develops a linguistic contrast. A human-focused thesis could test how
        that contrast affects processing and later reasoning. The final thesis direction remains open.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">A second case</h2>
      <p class="cw-p">
        The same question has an earlier face. A description built on a superordinate category term —
        <em>an animal perched on a branch</em> — leaves more than one candidate open, and a restricting
        detail narrows them. That narrowing is the same kind of event as evidence arriving after
        <em>might p</em>. What differs is which part of it the work so far measures: the typicality
        paper and the ProtoBias evaluation concern where the narrowing lands, while the modality work
        asks whether the possibility is held open in between and revised by later evidence. The second
        reading is a direction, not a result: the paper is explicit that it has an endpoint but no
        course, and the behavioural study has not begun.
      </p>
      <div class="cw-projs">
        <div class="cw-proj">
          <a class="cw-proj-title" href={projects.typicality.href}>{projects.typicality.title}</a>
          <p class="cw-proj-meta">{projects.typicality.status}</p>
          <p class="cw-proj-question">{projects.typicality.question}</p>
          <p class="cw-proj-desc">{projects.typicality.summary} {projects.typicality.connection}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-sec">
      <h2 class="cw-h2">Model research</h2>
      <p class="cw-p">
        These projects provide experience in controlled evaluation, competing explanations, and
        collaborative experiment design. Each has its own research question and evidential limits.
      </p>
      <div class="cw-projs">
        {background.map((p) => (
          <div class="cw-proj">
            <a class="cw-proj-title" href={p.href}>{p.title}</a>
            <p class="cw-proj-meta">{p.status}</p>
            <p class="cw-proj-question">{p.question}</p>
            <p class="cw-proj-desc">{p.summary} {p.connection}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .cw { max-width: 42rem; }
  .cw-h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; margin: 0 0 0.9rem; color: var(--color-text); }
  .cw-lede { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text); margin: 0 0 1rem; max-width: 40rem; }
  .cw-lede em { font-style: italic; }

  .cw-sec { margin-top: 2.8rem; }
  .cw-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-dim);
    margin: 0 0 1.2rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .cw-p { font-size: 0.96rem; line-height: 1.72; color: var(--color-text-muted); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .cw-p em { font-style: italic; color: var(--color-text); }

  .cw-ex {
    list-style: decimal;
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem 1rem 2.6rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: 0;
  }
  .cw-ex li { margin: 0.25rem 0; color: var(--color-text); line-height: 1.6; }
  .cw-ex em { font-style: italic; }

  .cw-crit { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding: 0; }
  .cw-crit li {
    padding: 0.55rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .cw-crit li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .cw-crit strong { color: var(--color-text); font-weight: 600; }

  .cw-qs { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .cw-qs { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .cw-q { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 0; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; }
  .cw-q-label { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 0.13em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-dim); margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .cw-q-text { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-text); margin: 0; }

  .cw-status {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  .cw-projs { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .cw-proj { padding: 1.05rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .cw-proj:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .cw-proj-top { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .cw-proj-title { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: none; }
  .cw-proj-title:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .cw-proj-title-plain { font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-text); }
  .cw-proj-title em, .cw-proj-title-plain em { font-style: italic; }
  .cw-proj-meta { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-dim); margin: 0.4rem 0 0.7rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .cw-proj-question { font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text); margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .cw-proj-current .cw-proj-meta { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .cw-proj-desc { font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-text-muted); margin: 0; max-width: 60ch; }
  .cw-proj-desc em { font-style: italic; }
  .cw-dep { margin: 0.9rem 0 0; padding-left: 0.9rem; border-left: 2px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 35%, transparent); font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.6; font-style: italic; color: var(--color-text); }
</style>
